3757: 
3758: The compact page is the indicated rational elliptic surface itself.
3759: Its line is \(M\), with square \(-d\), and
3760: \((dG)\cdot O=(d-2)/2\).  Every displayed mapping torus is therefore
3761: not diffeomorphic to \(S^1\times S^6\), by the same oriented
3762: mapping-torus detection used in P3K.10 and P3F.25.
3763: 
3764: There is also a restriction on additional smooth-site caps for these
3765: primitive sources.  After the actual finite-kernel cancellation of
3766: P3K.19, let their primitive cap data be
3767: \((m_i,n_i,\lambda_{ai},\lambda_{bi})\), with
3768: \(\gcd(m_i,n_i)=1\).  The exact orbit Mayer--Vietoris computation
3769: below gives, whenever its first-homology gluing matrix is unimodular,
3770: \[
3771:         H_2(Y;\mathbb Z)=\bigoplus_i(\mathbb Z/m_i)^2.       \tag{P3K.22.1}
3772: \]
3773: Consequently a sphere member with only such smooth-site modifications
3774: has \(m_i=1\) at every site.  For order-one sites the total gamma
3775: coefficient \(\sum n_i=\pm1\) gives sphere topology, including
3776: arbitrary integral source and delta collar coefficients.  The
3777: individual index table above specifies the one-site positive-gamma
3778: family; it does not identify uncomputed singular-source cap choices
3779: with this family.
3780: \end{statement}
3781: 
3782: \begin{proof}
3783: \par\medskip\noindent\textbf{The source automorphisms and the line lift.}\enspace
3784: P3F.1 constructs the source surfaces, and P3F.2 fixes their component
3785: markings and proves \(G\cdot O=0\).  Translation and inversion
3786: extend over their resolved singular fibres.  One direct proof keeps
3787: the actual rational maps.  On the generic Weierstrass fibre use its
3788: chord-and-tangent addition and \((x,y)\mapsto(x,-y)\).  Both preserve
3789: the invariant differential up to the constant sign of inversion.
3790: On a rational minimal elliptic surface the form
3791: \((dx/y)\wedge dt\) has canonical divisor \(-F_\infty\), after a
3792: base frame is chosen; the local minimal resolutions preserve this
3793: relative differential.  Resolve the graph of either rational map,
3794: with projections \(p,q:Z\to S\).  They are maps over the same base.
3795: Pulling back that differential gives
3796: \[
3797:  -p^*F_\infty+E_p=-q^*F_\infty+E_q,
3798:  \qquad p^*F_\infty=q^*F_\infty.
3799: \]
3800: Here the Jacobian discrepancy divisors have strictly positive
3801: coefficient on every exceptional curve of their respective
3802: compositions of point blowups.  Thus \(E_p=E_q\), so both
3803: projections contract exactly the same curves and connected fibres.
3804: The graph maps descend in both directions, giving mutually inverse
3805: holomorphic automorphisms of \(S\).  This proves the required
3806: extension, including II, III and IV fibres.  On an \(I_N\) chain
3807: the actual Tate map is multiplication by the section unit and a
3808: shift by its component label.  On III and IV, translation acts on
3809: the dense smooth parts by addition in the component groups of
3810: orders two and three; it therefore permutes the resolved components
3811: by those same labels.  Inversion conjugates translation by \(G\)
3812: to translation by \(-G\) on the whole surface.
3813: 
3814: The point \(dG\) is in the identity component at every reducible
3815: fibre in the table.  Thus \(M\) has multidegree zero there.
